---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const parabCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'a*x^2 + b*x + c', label: 'y = a·x² + b·x + c', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '0', label: 'y = 0', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.45)', width: 1.2 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'a', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.1, value: 1 },
    { name: 'b', label: 'b', min: -6, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: 0 },
    { name: 'c', label: 'c', min: -6, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: -3 },
  ],
  x: { min: -6, max: 6, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -8, max: 8, label: 'y' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 380,
};

const squareCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'x² + 6x − 7 = 0', note: 'A quadratic to solve. The strategy: build a perfect square on the left.' },
    { math: 'x² + 6x + 9 = 7 + 9', note: 'Add (b/2)² = 9 to both sides — half of 6, squared. Completing the square.' },
    { math: '(x + 3)² = 16', note: 'The left side is now a perfect square. This is the vertex form: a(x − h)² + k with vertex at (−3, −16).' },
    { math: 'x + 3 = ±4 → x = 1, −7', note: 'Two real roots. Check the discriminant: b² − 4ac = 36 + 28 = 64 > 0 — two roots, as promised.' },
  ],
};

const tangCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'a*x^2 + b*x + c', label: 'the parabola', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '(2*a*t + b)*(x - t) + (a*t^2 + b*t + c)', label: 'tangent at x = t', color: '#b42318', width: 1.6 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'a', min: -3, max: 3, step: 0.1, value: 1 },
    { name: 'b', label: 'b', min: -6, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: 0 },
    { name: 'c', label: 'c', min: -6, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: 0 },
    { name: 't', label: 'touch point x = t', min: -4, max: 4, step: 0.1, value: 2 },
  ],
  x: { min: -6, max: 6, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -8, max: 8, label: 'y' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/quadratics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Linear functions promise constant change; quadratics promise <em>constant acceleration</em> of
    that change — each step right adds more than the last, by the same increment every time.
    That is why anything thrown, dropped, braked or orbiting traces their curve: gravity adds
    the same velocity every second, and a position that gathers speed evenly is a quadratic in
    time. Grow a square garden and its area is the side length squared — another quadratic,
    and the one that named the family.
  </p>
  <p>
    The graph is a <strong>parabola</strong>, fully described by three numbers:
    <code>a</code> (how fast it opens — its sign decides up or down), <code>b</code> (tilts the
    axis of symmetry off centre), <code>c</code> (where it starts on the y-axis). Drag them
    below and watch the curve respond: <code>a</code> flexes the bowl, <code>b</code> slides
    its crest sideways, <code>c</code> lifts the whole thing. Slide <code>a</code> to exactly
    0 and the parabola degenerates into a straight line — a quadratic is not a different
    species from a linear function, but a linear function with one extra term.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the circled points are the roots — set a = 0 and the parabola degenerates into a line" widget="plotter" config={parabCfg} />

  <p>
    Watch the hollow markers where the curve crosses the dashed zero line — those are the
    <strong>roots</strong>, the solutions of the quadratic. Slide <code>c</code> upward until
    the curve lifts clear of the axis: the markers vanish. That disappearance is exactly what
    the discriminant measures, and we will make it precise below.
  </p>

  <h2>Solving quadratics: three tools for one job</h2>
  <p>
    Solving <code>ax² + bx + c = 0</code> means finding the roots — the x-values where the
    curve meets the axis — and you own three tools, each with its moment. <strong>Factorising</strong>
    is fastest when it works: x² + 6x − 7 = (x + 7)(x − 1), and since a product is zero only
    when a factor is zero, the roots leap out as x = 1 or x = −7. Look for two numbers that
    multiply to c and add to b — here +7 and −1 — and you are done in one line. Not every
    quadratic factorises over whole numbers, which is why the other two tools exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Completing the square</strong> is the honest, always-works method — turning any
    quadratic into a perfect square plus a remainder, which simultaneously solves it and
    reveals its shape:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="solving one honestly — completing the square" widget="equationStep" config={squareCfg} />

  <p>
    Notice the by-product hidden in step 3: the vertex form <code>a(x − h)² + k</code> reads
    the minimum (or maximum) point straight off the page, because a squared thing is never
    negative — the curve bottoms out exactly where the square vanishes. And the third tool,
    the <strong>quadratic formula</strong> x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, is nothing more than
    completing the square done once, for every quadratic at once, with letters standing where
    the numbers were. It is 9th-century Baghdad's finest export: al-Khwārizmī's solving
    procedures give us both the word <em>algebra</em> (from <em>al-jabr</em>, "restoring") and,
    through the Latinised form of his name, the word <em>algorithm</em>.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> after square-rooting, you must keep <em>both</em> signs.
  From (x + 3)² = 16 the answers are x + 3 = +4 <em>and</em> x + 3 = −4 — dropping the minus
  loses half the roots, the single most common quadratic error. The second: the ± in the
  formula means two answers unless the discriminant says otherwise; quoting "the answer"
  (singular) is usually wrong.</p>

  <h2>The discriminant: counting the crossings</h2>
  <p>
    The expression <code>b² − 4ac</code> under the square root is the
    <strong>discriminant</strong>, and it tells you the shape of the solution before you
    compute anything — because a negative number has no (real) square root. If b² − 4ac &gt;
    0, the curve crosses the axis twice: two real roots. If it equals 0, the curve just kisses
    the axis at one point — a <em>repeated root</em> sitting exactly at the vertex; y = x² −
    4x + 4 = (x − 2)² touches zero only at x = 2. If it is negative — y = x² + 2x + 5 has
    4 − 20 = −16 — there are no real roots and no crossings: the parabola floats entirely
    above (or below) the axis. Take the default curve in fig. 1: b² − 4ac = 0 + 12 = 12 &gt;
    0, two roots, and the two hollow markers confirm it. The discriminant is the arithmetic
    shadow of the geometry.
  </p>

  <h2>Vertex, symmetry and sketching</h2>
  <p>
    Every parabola is perfectly symmetric about a vertical line through its peak or trough,
    and that axis sits exactly midway between the roots — which gives a quick way to find it:
    the vertex has x-coordinate <code>−b / 2a</code>. Sketch y = x² − 4x + 3 by collecting four
    facts: it opens up (a = 1 &gt; 0); it crosses the y-axis at c = 3; factorising, x² − 4x +
    3 = (x − 1)(x − 3) gives roots at 1 and 3; and the vertex lies midway between them, at
    x = 2, where y = 4 − 8 + 3 = −1. Four points, one smooth symmetric bowl — a sketch any
    examiner will recognise. The vertex is also where quadratics earn money: if a company's
    profit is P = −2x² + 120x − 1,000 (x = units sold), the vertex at x = 30 is the profit
    maximum — every "find the best price" problem in a textbook is a vertex hunt in disguise.
  </p>

  <h2>Gradients on a curve: the tangent story</h2>
  <p>
    A line has one gradient; a curve has a different one at every point, and the gradient at a
    point is the gradient of the <em>tangent</em> — the straight line that just grazes the
    curve there. For a parabola the tangents behave with perfect regularity: at the vertex the
    tangent is horizontal (gradient 0), and it steepens steadily on either side. Differentiation
    (unit E2.12) makes this exact: for y = a·x² + b·x + c the gradient is 2a·x + b —
    itself a straight-line function of x. Slide the touch-point below and watch the tangent
    pivot through horizontal exactly at the vertex, then tilt ever more steeply:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="the tangent line — drag t along the curve and watch the gradient grow linearly" widget="plotter" config={tangCfg} />

  <p>
    Worked example: for y = x², the gradient at x = 3 is 2 × 3 = 6, and the tangent there is
    y = 6(x − 3) + 9. Notice the pleasing double linearity: the quadratic's <em>gradient</em>
    grows linearly, exactly as the lesson on linear functions promised — a parabola is a
    linear-gradient machine. This is also the physics connection made precise: the gradient of
    a distance–time parabola is the <em>instantaneous</em> velocity, the number the motion
    lesson approximated with tangents drawn by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>A full modelling problem: the thrown ball</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the parabola doing physics, with every tool on the page used in order. Throw a
    ball straight up at 20 m/s from ground level; gravity decelerates it by 9.8 m/s every
    second, and its height is <code>h = 20t − 4.9t²</code> — a quadratic with a = −4.9 (opens
    down), b = 20, c = 0. <em>When does it land?</em> Set h = 0 and solve: t(20 − 4.9t) = 0,
    giving t = 0 (the throw itself) or t = 20/4.9 ≈ 4.08 s. <em>How high does it go?</em> The
    vertex sits at t = −b/2a = −20/(−9.8) ≈ 2.04 s — exactly half the flight time, the
    symmetry of the parabola made physical — with height 20(2.04) − 4.9(2.04)² ≈ 20.4 m.
    <em>What is its velocity at the top?</em> Zero — and the gradient argument proves it: at
    the vertex the tangent is horizontal, which is the algebraic way of saying the ball
    stops rising an instant before falling. Notice how each property you learned as algebra
    reappears as physics: roots as launch and landing, vertex as apex, symmetry axis as the
    mid-flight instant, negative a as gravity pulling the bowl downward. Galileo's
    contemporaries called this curve the trajectory of "mixed motion" — uniform horizontal
    glide plus steady fall — and it took the quadratic to prove the mix produces exactly
    this shape, never a peak and never an arc of any other kind.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same recipe solves the revenue problems: profit = revenue − cost is a quadratic
    when price falls with volume (<code>P = x(100 − x) − fixed</code>), and "maximise" is
    always the vertex. One equation, two professions.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing the tool, and the exam traps</h2>
  <p>
    A short decision tree serves every quadratic. Try <strong>factorising</strong> first
    (fast, exact); if the numbers resist, check the <strong>discriminant</strong> — negative
    means no real roots and you can stop; otherwise use the <strong>formula</strong> (always
    works, watch the signs: a negative <code>b</code> flipped inside <code>−b</code> is the
    classic slip) or <strong>complete the square</strong> if the question also asks for the
    vertex. When <code>a ≠ 1</code>, factorising gets harder (2x² + 5x − 3 = (2x − 1)(x + 3),
    found by trial or the AC method: multiply 2 × −3 = −6, find two numbers multiplying to
    −6 and adding to 5 — that's 6 and −1 — then split the middle term), and the formula
    earns its keep. Two traps account for most lost marks: forgetting the ± after
    square-rooting, and dividing through by <code>x</code> in an equation like x² = 3x —
    which throws away the root x = 0. Factor out instead: x(x − 3) = 0 gives both answers,
    0 and 3. Quadratics punish shortcuts; they reward the checklist.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The parabola's reflection property is its signature in the built world: any ray entering
    parallel to the axis bounces off the curve and passes through a single point, the
    <em>focus</em>. That is the entire design of satellite dishes, radio telescopes, car
    headlights (run backwards — a bulb at the focus emits parallel beams) and solar furnaces,
    which melt metal with sunlight collected over hundreds of square metres. Apollonius of
    Perga catalogued the conic sections around 200 BCE with pure geometry, no algebra, and
    Galileo confirmed in 1638 that projectiles fly in parabolas — the curve has been doing
    physics for two millennia before its equation was written.
  </p>
  <p>
    The negative discriminant, meanwhile, is a doorway. "No real roots" bothered 16th-century
    algebraists until Cardano's circle (1545) started taking √ of negative numbers seriously
    as intermediate bookkeeping that gave <em>correct final answers</em> for real problems.
    The bookkeeping hardened into the complex numbers, with i = √(−1), now as fundamental to
    physics as any real quantity — every quantum-mechanical wavefunction lives there. And the
    family keeps yielding structure: x² − x − 1 = 0 has the golden ratio φ ≈ 1.618 as a root;
    a quadratic's two roots always satisfy root₁ + root₂ = −b/a and root₁ × root₂ = c/a
    (check with x² + 6x − 7: 1 + (−7) = −6 ✓, 1 × (−7) = −7 ✓) — relationships you can use to
    check answers in seconds. Simple curve, deep roots.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> al-Khwārizmī, <em>The Compendious Book on Calculation by Completion and Balancing</em> (c. 820); Apollonius, <em>Conics</em> (c. 200 BCE); OpenStax, <em>Elementary Algebra</em> and <em>College Algebra</em>; Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics 0580 syllabus, sections E2.5, E2.10–E2.12.</p>
</TopicPage>
